3.1 Introduction to Node.js
Node.js is an open-source, cross-platform JavaScript runtime built on Chrome's V8 JavaScript engine.
It allows JavaScript to run outside the browser — on the server side.
Key Characteristics of Node.js📌
Asynchronous & Non-Blocking: Operations (I/O, file, network) don't block the thread▸
Event-Driven Architecture: Uses an event loop to handle multiple connections efficiently▸
Single-Threaded: One thread handles all requests via the event loop (unlike multi-threaded ▸
servers)
Fast: Built on V8 engine — compiles JavaScript to machine code▸
npm Ecosystem: Access to 2+ million packages via Node Package Manager▸
Node.js Event Loop
The event loop is what makes Node.js non-blocking. It continuously checks for pending callbacks and
executes them without blocking the main thread.
// Synchronous (Blocking)
const data = fs.readFileSync('file.txt'); // blocks until done
console.log(data);
// Asynchronous (Non-Blocking)
fs.readFile('file.txt', (err, data) => { // doesn't block
if (err) throw err;
console.log(data);
});
console.log('This runs BEFORE file is read!'); // executes immediately
// Using Promises (modern approach)
const data = await fs.promises.readFile('file.txt');
